00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:39.384
Leave this topic for now and just pay
attention on my words

00:03:40.082 --> 00:03:42.262
I've (fruit = "Mango")

00:03:43.597 --> 00:03:46.709
And I've printed 'len1' here

00:03:47.364 --> 00:03:48.478
Or 'mangoLen'

00:03:48.964 --> 00:03:51.757
I can give it any name

00:03:52.514 --> 00:03:56.173
I've named it 'mangoLen'
so I'll get '5' printed here

00:03:56.546 --> 00:03:59.159
Now if I want to print the starting
2 letter of 'fruit'

00:03:59.332 --> 00:04:02.252
So I'll write "fruit" and then I'll write...

00:04:02.798 --> 00:04:03.464
..print(fruit)

00:04:03.851 --> 00:04:05.469
And I'll add this 'square bracket notation'

00:04:05.494 --> 00:04:07.642
Make sure you don't use parenthesis

00:04:07.735 --> 00:04:09.628
We use parenthesis for 'functions'

00:04:09.941 --> 00:04:12.160
And for slicing we use ' Square Brackets[ ] '

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:16.033
On your keyboard 'Square Brackets' are
on the right of letter 'P'

00:04:16.252 --> 00:04:17.679
Look for them on your keyboard

00:04:17.947 --> 00:04:21.846
And let's suppose if I write
" print(fruits[0:4]) "

00:04:21.871 --> 00:04:24.048
So I'll get the first 4 characters

00:04:24.082 --> 00:04:31.320
Basically from this line of code I want to
specify that give me Index[0] [1] [2] [3]

00:04:31.497 --> 00:04:34.244
You won't get the 4th index

00:04:34.536 --> 00:04:37.868
You'll only get index from [0] to [3]

00:04:37.893 --> 00:04:39.870
[0] [1] [2] [3], you'll only get these 4 characters

00:04:39.895 --> 00:04:43.517
I'll run this, and the output will
be [M] [A] [N] [G]

00:04:44.135 --> 00:04:45.968
What if I don't add this [0]

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:47.665
I can skip this [0]

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.898
If I don't add this [0]
so what Python will do is...

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:54.454
It'll automatically consider it as [0]
if I skip this [0]

00:04:54.624 --> 00:04:56.190
So this is the advantage of Python

00:04:56.512 --> 00:05:00.340
Now what if I want index from [1] ?

00:05:00.365 --> 00:05:02.828
So I'll write [1:4]

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.424
So I'll get [A] [N] [G]

00:05:05.474 --> 00:05:07.047
Look I got [A] [N] &amp; [G]

00:05:07.671 --> 00:05:12.679
But if I write it like " print(fruit[:4]) "

00:05:12.842 --> 00:05:15.419
So Python is smart enough

00:05:15.914 --> 00:05:17.427
That it'll automatically consider
this blank as [0]

00:05:17.831 --> 00:05:20.444
And you'll get [M] [A] [N] [G] as output

00:05:21.021 --> 00:05:24.233
And if I remove this [4] from here
so what Python will do?

00:05:24.497 --> 00:05:27.097
It'll automatically add the
'Length of Fruit'

00:05:27.122 --> 00:05:28.786
What is the 'Length of fruit'?
It's 5

00:05:29.045 --> 00:05:29.998
So it'll add [5]

00:05:30.023 --> 00:05:31.992
Look if I don't add [5] here

00:05:32.979 --> 00:05:36.466
Even then the output is 'Mango'
and if I add [5] so I'll get 'Mango'

00:05:37.298 --> 00:05:39.278
So this is how slicing works

00:05:39.604 --> 00:05:41.903
Now I want to tell you one more
thing about 'Slicing'

00:05:41.928 --> 00:05:43.231
'Negative Slicing'

00:05:43.385 --> 00:05:44.991
If I change this to [-1]

00:05:45.016 --> 00:05:49.699
And I'll change this to [0] because if I don't write
anything even then it'll be considered as [0]

00:05:49.975 --> 00:05:51.995
And what if I write [-3] here

00:05:52.512 --> 00:05:54.032
So look, what the output will be

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.174
And I'll tell you why the output is this

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.546
The output is "Ma"

00:05:57.584 --> 00:05:58.830
Why's that?

00:06:00.245 --> 00:06:02.379
So firstly let's understand how
Python interprets it

00:06:02.691 --> 00:06:03.851
This [-3]...

00:06:04.135 --> 00:06:05.432
Python interpreter automatically...

00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:10.959
...Adds the 'Length of fruit'

00:06:11.369 --> 00:06:14.208
What is the 'Length of Fruit'?
It's 5

00:06:14.233 --> 00:06:15.989
We're already familiar, it is 5

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:17.590
'Length of Fruit' is 5

00:06:17.914 --> 00:06:19.634
And (5-3) is 2

00:06:19.802 --> 00:06:25.954
And [0:2] means [0]M  [1]A

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:29.283
So it's 'M' on Index[0] and 'A' on Index[1]

00:06:29.387 --> 00:06:30.567
So "Ma" is being printed

00:06:30.869 --> 00:06:32.589
The output of this, will also be "Ma"

00:06:33.284 --> 00:06:35.984
Let me tell you for your clarity,
if I write this...

00:06:36.944 --> 00:06:38.557
Or this...
The output will be the same

00:06:38.645 --> 00:06:40.199
The output will be "Ma" both the times

00:06:40.565 --> 00:06:42.958
Because Python automatically considered
this as 'Length of Fruit'

00:06:43.286 --> 00:06:45.186
What about 'Negative Slicing' here

00:06:45.241 --> 00:06:47.921
What will be printed if I write [-1] here

00:06:48.907 --> 00:06:52.407
1 sec, let me write this in a new line
so that you won't be confused

00:06:53.066 --> 00:06:55.789
If I write nothing here so this &amp;
this are equivalent

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:59.976
Because for 'Negative Slicing' Python automatically 
consider this as 'Length of Fruit'

00:07:00.522 --> 00:07:02.702
And what if I change this to [-1]

00:07:03.583 --> 00:07:04.598
So look.....

00:07:04.927 --> 00:07:10.129
Let me comment this out so you'll know
what exactly is printing here

00:07:10.461 --> 00:07:12.867
If I tweak it like this, if I change this to [-1]

00:07:12.869 --> 00:07:15.455
So look, 5 has been printed here
and nothing was printed here

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.319
Now let's understand what's this

00:07:17.679 --> 00:07:19.592
So I've written
" print(fruit[-1:len(fruit)-3]) "

00:07:19.629 --> 00:07:22.443
And here I only have written -1

00:07:22.804 --> 00:07:26.364
So Python interpreter automatically
adds the 'Length of Fruit'

00:07:27.023 --> 00:07:30.296
So python interpreter will interprets this
in such a way that "It'll say...

00:07:30.546 --> 00:07:32.913
...'Length of Fruit' is 5 so (5-1) is 4"

00:07:33.341 --> 00:07:35.167
And (5-3) is 2

00:07:35.205 --> 00:07:37.872
So there's no sense in [4:2]

00:07:38.386 --> 00:07:41.833
However if I reverse this, which means if
I change this [-1] with [-3]

00:07:42.694 --> 00:07:44.901
And if I change this [-3] with [-1]

00:07:45.282 --> 00:07:47.969
So what will happen?
And there's no need for me to add 'Length of Fruit'

00:07:48.384 --> 00:07:51.804
I was just telling you that Python
automatically adds 'Length of Fruit'

00:07:52.316 --> 00:07:53.342
If I run this...

00:07:54.158 --> 00:07:57.971
So you'll see the output is "ng"
Now let's decode, why?

00:07:58.339 --> 00:08:01.592
Firstly Python interprets it as
(Length of Fruit -3)

00:08:01.924 --> 00:08:03.424
Which is (5-3)=2

00:08:03.611 --> 00:08:04.917
So here the value will be [2]

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.904
And here the value will be [4]

00:08:08.289 --> 00:08:10.483
So it'll start from the index[2]
to the index[3]

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:14.539
Do you remember that Python starts
from here and ends here

00:08:14.881 --> 00:08:17.474
So [0] [1] [2] which means the
output will be " n "

00:08:17.664 --> 00:08:19.318
And then index[3] will be printed i.e. " g "

00:08:19.684 --> 00:08:22.639
The output will be " ng " and
index[4] will be ignored

00:08:22.664 --> 00:08:23.630
I already have told you

00:08:23.883 --> 00:08:26.643
Including this but not including this

00:08:26.927 --> 00:08:28.194
Now I want to write this down

00:08:28.522 --> 00:08:29.835
I'd like to write this

00:08:30.161 --> 00:08:30.542
That....

00:08:31.588 --> 00:08:34.881
"Including 0 but not 4"

00:08:35.551 --> 00:08:40.750
In-fact I'll write this here too so that you won't be
confused "if there's a special case with [0]"

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.803
No man! There's no special case with 'zero'
I was just....

00:08:44.313 --> 00:08:47.040
If I paste this here so the behaviour
will be same for [1]

00:08:47.464 --> 00:08:51.024
As you can see when I wrote [0:4]
so it includes 0 but not 4

00:08:51.274 --> 00:08:53.794
Including 1 but not 4, i.e. from 1 to 3

00:08:53.871 --> 00:08:55.151
And this is the index

00:08:55.364 --> 00:08:57.543
Which means it's the number to
start counting from [0]

00:08:57.568 --> 00:08:58.970
If you start counting from [0]

00:08:59.261 --> 00:09:00.827
Which means in place of [0], it's ' m '

00:09:01.236 --> 00:09:03.368
In place of [1], it's ' a '
In place of [2], it's ' n '

00:09:03.449 --> 00:09:06.441
In place of [3], it's ' g '
And in place of [4], it's ' o '

00:09:07.328 --> 00:09:09.134
So this is how you can do 'String Slicing'

